        {
          "cwe_id": "CWE-770",
          "name": "Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling"
        }
      ],
      "description": "### Summary\r\n\r\n`Plug.Parsers.MULTIPART`, the multipart request-body parser in the `plug` Elixir library that handles file uploads and multipart forms, does not charge its `:length` budget against file uploads. An unauthenticated remote attacker who can reach any multipart endpoint can send a single request composed of many empty-body file parts that stays well under the configured `:length` limit (8 MB by default), exhausting inodes and disk and growing memory (denial of service).\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\n`Plug.Parsers.MULTIPART` (`lib/plug/parsers/multipart.ex`) pops the operator-configured `:length` option and self-manages a cumulative `limit` variable that is supposed to bound per-request resource use. The flaw is in what `limit` is not actually charged for file uploads, allowing an attacker to stream an arbitrary number of such parts in one request. The result is an unbounded number of temporary files (inode/disk exhaustion), plus unbounded retained `Plug.Upload` structs and accumulator entries (memory growth). The only external mitigation is a reverse-proxy request-size cap, which lies outside the parser. The fix charges `limit` for header bytes and enforces a bound on part count, erroring with `:too_large` once exceeded.\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\nAn unauthenticated attacker can bypass the `:length` budget that operators depend on and, with a single HTTP request, force a Plug application to create an unbounded number of temporary files and retain an unbounded number of `Plug.Upload` structs, exhausting host inodes and disk and growing memory. Any application using `Plug.Parsers` with the `:multipart` parser (the standard path for file uploads and multipart forms) is affected, degrading or crashing the service for all users.\r\n\r\n### References\r\n\r\n* Introduction commit: https://github.com/elixir-plug/plug/commit/c52b2f32c90bccd718202bafccb5f95594e30183\r\n* Patch commit: https://github.com/elixir-plug/plug/commit/981597d3a4271ede64373c7a731702a42c500dd6\r\n",
